<subsection>
<title>Class Definition</title>
<p>
      A Java program consists of one or more class definitions. This is somewhat unique to the Java 
      programming language. In fact, other languages have a much more simple "Hello World" example (typically only one line of code). 
      </p>
      <p>In the <c>HelloWorld</c> example,
      we are defining the <c>HelloWorld</c> class. Other classes are used in the program (notably System),
      but the significance of this will be explored later. 
</p>
<p>
      As the <c>HelloWorld</c> program's comments indicate,
      a class definition has two parts:
      a <em>class header</em> and a <em>class body</em>.
      There are more complex class headers than the header
      in our initial example, but we'll start simple. 
      The class header for the <c>HelloWorld</c> class is:
</p>
<program language="java"><input>
	public class HelloWorld 
</input></program>
<p>
	  The purpose of the header is to give the class its name (<c>HelloWorld</c>), identify its 
	  accessibility (<c>public</c> as opposed to <c>private</c> -- more on these <em>access modifiers</em> later), and establish
	  other properties of the class (we will also explore some of these later -- none are present in this example 
	  so we are merely using the default properties). In our case, the header begins with the optional access modifier, <c>public</c>, which 
	  declares that this class can be accessed by any other classes, and the next part of the declaration identifies the name of the class, <c>HelloWorld</c>. 
</p>
<p>
	  Let's take another look at the example code (<xref ref="lstng_helloworld"></xref>). The class's body, which is enclosed within curly brackets, { and }, 
	  contains the declaration and definition of the elements that make up what are called the <em>members</em> of the class.
	  Notice that the opening brace is on the same line as the method header. This is a convention many (probably about half) of Java
	  programmers follow. Others will begin the class body on the next line by moving the { to the beginning of the next line. 
	  Both choices are considered acceptable style as long as the programmer is consistent with this choice (and consistent 
	  with the same choice of other programmers on a project). 
	  </p>
	  <p>
	  Now let's talk a little about class members. We will later see several types of class members, but for now, in our current example, 
	  we only see one: The main method. You see a similar block structure for this definition as with the class definition. We will discuss
	  each element of this method definition in time, but for now understand that it is, as written, the default starting point for a 
	  complete Java program. Inside of the method is a <em>statement</em>. We will further examine statements soon, later in this section.
	  </p>
</subsection>

